{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfe3Sq5AvWUcTffUFVsfa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've rewritten all three files for this repair slot, and the new candidate passes the self-check. But that check doesn't test convergence, so I can't confirm it fixes the convergence failure that got the first attempt rejected.\n\n**Why the first attempt failed to converge:** it added a free-floating \"attentiveness\" factor that fed into sensitivity, pattern-detection gain and guessing rate. The factor's three weights could flip sign together, and they traded off against each person's separate spread terms. That made the fit unidentifiable, which matches the rejection (R-hat 1.57, bulk ESS 7).\n\n**The fix:** I dropped the separate factor. Each person's own switching-sensitivity deviate now also shifts their log pattern-detection gain and their logit guessing rate, by two fitted weights that can be positive or negative (`link_gain`, `link_lapse`). That deviate is already pinned down by how the person handles switching, so the signs are identified and only two parameters are added to the incumbent.\n\n- **Name:** `sensitivity_linked_consistency`\n- **Hypothesis:** a refinement of `personal_pattern_detection_gain`. People who judge alternation carefully also notice designed patterns and rarely guess, because these traits are correlated rather than independent. It targets the critique's one discrepancy that survives FDR: people differ more than the model predicts in how often they agree with the majority.\n\n**Self-check:** it prints `OK`. The 100-draw, single-chain smoke fit gave an ELPD-LOO of −3442.8, against −3451.1 for the incumbent. That's only a smoke-fit number and not a fair comparison. The check also warned that the smoke fit's PSIS-LOO was unreliable (9.3% of trials had a high Pareto k), and that it couldn't report R-hat with one chain.\n\n**Still a risk:** I didn't run a multi-chain fit because this session had one CPU and the smoke fit alone took about five minutes. If people's switching sensitivities turn out to barely vary, the deviate stops being pinned down and the fit could fail again. The incumbent did converge with that same sensitivity term. The novelty gate also hasn't been checked. A second rejection is final for this slot.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run1/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop/iter_4/candidate_4_repair_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2380,"cache_read_input_tokens":108703,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2380,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e94db6b-0636-4cda-a459-8d57ae1853cd","uuid":"55fedddf-1a8a-4e08-b49f-46945d798e92","timestamp":"2026-10-02T20:29:39.092Z","request_id":"req_011Cfe3SpgcQMYqUqg5nYvSv"}
